1
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:50,580
CONVERSATION 1
Good morning and welcome to "People in the News".

2
00:00:50,580 --> 00:01:00,840
With me today is Megan Brown, an environmental activist whose controversial new book Beyond Recycling is making headlines.

3
00:01:00,840 --> 00:01:03,520
Hi Brian, thanks for having me today.

4
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:08,500
I'm excited to explain to the audience what my book is really about.

5
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:13,980
Critics of your book assert that you're trying to force radical changes on the entire country.

6
00:01:13,980 --> 00:01:22,000
Some claim that you want to force everyone to eat a vegetarian diet and make private transport illegal.

7
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,160
I'm aware of those claims but they simply aren't true.

8
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:30,900
People who haven't read the book are making assumptions about my arguments.

9
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:47,680
They know I'm a vegetarian, that I don't wear leather or fur and that I always use public transportation, so they're depicting me as a radical animal rights activist and environmentalist, determined to force my beliefs on others.

10
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:50,880
But don't you want others to adopt your practices?

11
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:54,660
You've campaigned for animal rights and the environment for decades.

12
00:01:54,660 --> 00:02:06,080
I'd love it if people chose to live as I do, but my life choices are based on my personal convictions, they aren't my recommendations for others who don't share those convictions.

13
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:20,320
Well, in this excerpt from your book, you argue that meat consumption and private transport are devastating the environment and that the best choices for the planet are vegetarian diets and public transport.

14
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:27,660
I did write that, but those are examples of what I call best practices not what I'm actually suggesting.

15
00:02:27,660 --> 00:02:36,320
In my guidelines for saving the environment, I suggest modest changes, like eating vegetarian meals two days a week.

16
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:43,180
You also endorse high taxes on meat and other animal products and increase taxes on gasoline.

17
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:48,100
Those taxes could force poor people to adopt your life choices.

18
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:53,520
But the taxes I suggest aren't that high, less than 3% only.

19
00:02:53,520 --> 00:03:06,440
Plus the money generated would be allocated to environmental protection which benefits everyone.

20
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:23,123
Q1. What do we learn about the woman's new book?

21
00:03:23,123 --> 00:03:41,660
Q2. What do some critics say about the author of the book?

22
00:03:41,660 --> 00:03:59,980
Q3. What does the woman claim about the diet changes she suggested?

23
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:18,800
Q4. What does the woman say about her suggested tax increase?

24
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:27,040
CONVERSATION 2
With me in the studio today is Miss Jane Logan, author of the new book Secrets to Success.

25
00:04:27,040 --> 00:04:31,940
She claims to have uncovered how people achieve success.

26
00:04:31,940 --> 00:04:37,800
So Miss Logan, in your book you claim that successful people have many things in common.

27
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:41,760
For instance, they know their strengths when pursuing a goal.

28
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:43,040
That's right.

29
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:48,300
They also tend to be motivated by a negative or positive life event.

30
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:53,080
They credit their success to having someone in their life who believes in them.

31
00:04:53,080 --> 00:04:58,720
You also write that there are a number of different factors related to success.

32
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:06,200
And while successful people are driven to achieve their goals, the ultra successful have even greater ambition.

33
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:14,560
Yes, greater ambition as well as a burning desire to be the best of the best is also a common characteristic.

34
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:26,000
Right, so those who are determined don't see obstacles as something that prevents success, but mere inconveniences that need to be overcome.

35
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:26,940
Absolutely.

36
00:05:26,940 --> 00:05:37,920
Successful people are also optimistic as it is important to stay positive while being aware of obstacles that can deter us from achieving our goals.

37
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:39,440
That's a good point.

38
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:43,280
All too often people give up at the first hurdle.

39
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:47,420
Would you say then that most successful people make it all by themselves?

40
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:49,500
Not exactly.

41
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:55,220
They are usually good at cooperating with people and understanding the needs of others.

42
00:05:55,220 --> 00:05:58,360
So people will be willing to help them, I guess.

43
00:05:58,360 --> 00:06:08,180
That's correct and this often leads to a great deal of mutual respect whether it's with a colleague, an assistant or even a receptionist.

44
00:06:08,180 --> 00:06:12,520
Most successful people I know are very passionate about their work.

45
00:06:12,520 --> 00:06:16,140
Would you say that passion is the single biggest key to success?

46
00:06:16,140 --> 00:06:18,300
Not entirely.

47
00:06:18,300 --> 00:06:24,700
There's a prerequisite that is you have to work in an environment that nurtures passion.

48
00:06:24,700 --> 00:06:31,545
If that exists, success will follow.

49
00:06:31,545 --> 00:06:50,940
Q5. What has the woman revealed in her book?

50
00:06:50,940 --> 00:07:09,200
Q6. What do successful people attribute their achievements to?

51
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:39,809
Q7. What do successful people do when faced with difficulties?

52
00:07:39,809 --> 00:08:13,820
Q8. What is one prerequisite for passionate work according to the woman?

53
00:08:13,820 --> 00:08:18,200
PASSAGE 1
That saying "blind as a bat" simply isn't correct.

54
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:30,460
The truth is that all 1,100 bat species can see and often their vision is pretty good, although not as excellent as other night hunting animals.

55
00:08:30,460 --> 00:08:39,340
There are two main groups of bats which are believed to have evolved independently of each other but both from a common ancestor.

56
00:08:39,340 --> 00:08:49,620
The first group known as mega bats are mostly medium sized or large bats who eat fruits, flowers and sometimes small animals or fish.

57
00:08:49,620 --> 00:08:54,100
These species have distinctive visual centers and big eyes.

58
00:08:54,100 --> 00:08:58,140
They use senses of vision and smell to capture their prey.

59
00:08:58,140 --> 00:09:04,720
For example, flying foxes not only see well during daylight but can also distinguish colors.

60
00:09:04,720 --> 00:09:12,100
They actually rely on their daylight vision and cannot fly during the night with no moonlight.

61
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:18,420
The second group called micro bats are smaller in size and mostly eat insects.

62
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:24,160
These species use echolocation to find their way and identify food.

63
00:09:24,160 --> 00:09:31,500
Scientists have proven that despite their poorly developed small eyes, these bats still can see during the day.

64
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:40,460
When we consider the nightly lifestyle of these bats, we will see they have to be sensitive to the changing light levels.

65
00:09:40,460 --> 00:09:44,460
Because this is how they sense when to start hunting.

66
00:09:44,460 --> 00:09:57,020
Moreover, vision is used by micro bats to travel over long distances beyond the range of echolocation, so the truth is there are no bats which are naturally blind.

67
00:09:57,020 --> 00:10:17,882
Some species use their sense of hearing more than their eyes as a matter of adaptation to a particular lifestyle, but their eyes are still functional.

68
00:10:17,882 --> 00:10:30,720
Q9. What do we learn about mega bats?

69
00:10:30,720 --> 00:10:49,280
Q10. How do micro bats find their way and identify food?

70
00:10:49,280 --> 00:11:10,200
Q11. Why do some species of bats use their sense of hearing more than their eyes?

71
00:11:10,200 --> 00:11:17,220
PASSAGE 2
A study has found that educational TV shows come with an added lesson that influences the child's behavior.

72
00:11:17,220 --> 00:11:25,800
Children spending more time watching educational programs increase their emotional aggression toward other children.

73
00:11:25,800 --> 00:11:30,580
This shows that children can learn the educational lesson that was intended.

74
00:11:30,580 --> 00:11:35,180
However, they're also learning other things along the way.

75
00:11:35,180 --> 00:11:44,480
This unintended impact has to do with the portrayal of conflict in media and how preschool age children comprehend that conflict.

76
00:11:44,480 --> 00:11:50,420
TV and movie producers often incorporate an element of bad behavior.

77
00:11:50,420 --> 00:11:55,060
This is to teach children a lesson at the end of the program.

78
00:11:55,060 --> 00:12:00,420
Educational shows have pro-education and pro-social goals.

79
00:12:00,420 --> 00:12:07,460
However, conflict between characters is often depicted with characters being unkind to each other.

80
00:12:07,460 --> 00:12:12,120
Or they may use emotionally aggressive tactics with each other.

81
00:12:12,120 --> 00:12:16,760
Preschool children really don't get the moral of the story.

82
00:12:16,760 --> 00:12:22,700
That's because it requires that they understand how all the parts of the show fit together.

83
00:12:22,700 --> 00:12:28,580
You need pretty complicated cognitive skills and memory skills to be able to do that.

84
00:12:28,580 --> 00:12:32,200
These are still developing in young children.

85
00:12:32,200 --> 00:12:35,880
However, parents shouldn't completely constrain children's viewing.

86
00:12:35,880 --> 00:12:42,760
Parents should instead watch with their kids and help them to understand the plot.

87
00:12:42,760 --> 00:12:48,240
Parents can comment along the way and then explain the message at the end.

88
00:12:48,240 --> 00:12:53,680
They should explain how certain types of behavior were not appropriate.

89
00:12:53,680 --> 00:13:08,620
This will help children interpret and get the message and help them learn to watch the show for those messages.

90
00:13:08,620 --> 00:13:28,360
Q12. What does the passage say about children watching educational programs?

91
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:48,600
Q13. Why can't preschool children get the moral conveyed in the TV programs?

92
00:13:48,600 --> 00:14:06,700
Q14. What does the passage suggest parents do?

93
00:14:06,700 --> 00:14:51,080
Q15. What should parents do right after watching the TV program?

94
00:14:51,080 --> 00:15:00,660
RECORDING 1
While it has long been a practice in Asian countries, many people in Western countries have yet to embrace the no shoes in the house rule.

95
00:15:00,660 --> 00:15:07,600
For many in those countries, wiping their shoes before going inside is regarded as sufficient.

96
00:15:07,600 --> 00:15:19,640
After all, they may think a little dirt doesn't hurt anyone, but I can give you several good reasons why people should remove their shoes before going inside their homes.

97
00:15:19,640 --> 00:15:21,600
Bacteria are everywhere.

98
00:15:21,600 --> 00:15:28,460
They collect on your shoes when you walk along sidewalks, public toilet floors and even office carpets.

99
00:15:28,460 --> 00:15:37,060
In one study conducted at an American university, researchers collected microscopic germs from footwear.

100
00:15:37,060 --> 00:15:45,500
They found that up to 421,000 units of bacteria can collect on the outside of a single shoe.

101
00:15:45,500 --> 00:15:49,920
And it only takes a little bit of dirt to damage your timber floors.

102
00:15:49,920 --> 00:15:55,660
The more dirt you track in, the more it scratches and the more often you have to clean.

103
00:15:55,660 --> 00:16:00,260
The extra scrubbing will harm your floors over time.

104
00:16:00,260 --> 00:16:07,980
Shoes can also leave marks and scratches on floors, especially high heels or shoes with pointy or hard parts.

105
00:16:07,980 --> 00:16:10,740
These can dent and scratch your floor.

106
00:16:10,740 --> 00:16:21,140
And if you live in an apartment building, removing your noisy shoes is the polite thing to do out of consideration for your downstairs neighbors.

107
00:16:21,140 --> 00:16:24,580
Walking around with bare feet is actually better for your feet.

108
00:16:24,580 --> 00:16:32,840
It strengthens the muscles in your feet though many people are accustomed to wearing shoes during all their waking hours.

109
00:16:32,840 --> 00:16:40,220
The more time you spend wearing shoes, the more likely you are to incur foot injuries as a result.

110
00:16:40,220 --> 00:16:45,740
And if people aren't yet convinced by my arguments, I can give one final reason.

111
00:16:45,740 --> 00:16:55,280
The dust and toxic chemicals you bring into your house via your shoes can deteriorate the air quality in your home.

112
00:16:55,280 --> 00:17:05,160
Toxic chemicals are everywhere in our lives: insect killing chemicals used on public grass areas, cleaning chemicals on the floors of public areas.

113
00:17:05,160 --> 00:17:11,900
By kicking off your shoes before you enter the home, you are denying entry to these harmful chemicals.

114
00:17:11,900 --> 00:17:31,260
Given the amount of time we spend in classrooms and the number of shoes that pass through them every day, you may well understand me if I were to propose a no shoes in the classroom rule.

115
00:17:31,260 --> 00:17:50,340
Q16. Why don't many Westerners take off shoes before entering a house?

116
00:17:50,340 --> 00:18:10,420
Q17. What is the finding of one study by researchers at an American university?

117
00:18:10,420 --> 00:18:31,020
Q18. What is the final reason the speaker gives for removing shoes before entering a house?

118
00:18:31,020 --> 00:18:35,900
RECORDING 2
Emotions are an essential and inseparable part of our consciousness.

119
00:18:35,900 --> 00:18:42,620
They are part of a built-in mechanism which allows us to cope with the ups and downs of our lives both physically and mentally.

120
00:18:42,620 --> 00:18:51,360
When we hide our emotions and our true feelings, we stop being genuine, spontaneous and authentic in our relationships.

121
00:18:51,360 --> 00:18:58,120
When we put on cultivated and polished faces in the company of others, we stop being true to ourselves.

122
00:18:58,120 --> 00:19:05,140
Socially, it may be a good tactic to hide our true emotions but morally it is also a breach of faith and trust.

123
00:19:05,140 --> 00:19:15,460
Although humans are more advanced and intelligent, animals are more genuine and authentic in their behavior and responses than human beings.

124
00:19:15,460 --> 00:19:20,680
The more educated we are, the less transparent and reliable we become.

125
00:19:20,680 --> 00:19:24,980
We are drawn to our pets because pets do not lie.

126
00:19:24,980 --> 00:19:31,440
One of the first steps in dealing with emotions such as anger or fear is to acknowledge them as normal and human.

127
00:19:31,440 --> 00:19:40,920
There is nothing sinful or immoral about being emotional unless your emotions make you inhuman, insensitive and cruel to others.

128
00:19:40,920 --> 00:19:48,260
Feelings of guilt associated with emotions are more devastating and damaging than the experience of emotion itself.

129
00:19:48,260 --> 00:19:58,480
So when you deal with the problem of emotions, you should learn not only how to control them but also how to accept them and manage the guilt and anguish arising from them.

130
00:19:58,480 --> 00:20:06,720
Our brains are made up of a primitive inner core and a more evolved and rational outer core.

131
00:20:06,720 --> 00:20:17,260
Most of the time, the rational part of the mind controls the information coming from the primitive core and makes its own decisions as to what to do and how to respond.

132
00:20:17,260 --> 00:20:29,820
However, during critical situations, especially when a threat is perceived, the outer core loses control and fails to regulate the impulses and instinctive responses coming from the primitive brain.

133
00:20:29,820 --> 00:20:37,640
As a result, we let disturbing thoughts and emotions arise in our consciousness and surrender to our primitive behavior.

134
00:20:37,640 --> 00:20:49,880
In times of emotional turmoil, remember that emotions arise because your sensors are wired to the primitive part of your mind which is self-regulated, autonomous and spontaneous.

135
00:20:49,880 --> 00:20:54,020
Your rational mind does not always deal with the messages coming from it effectively.

136
00:20:54,020 --> 00:21:04,100
These messages are part of your survival mechanism and should not be stifled simply because emotions are unhealthy and betray your weaknesses.

137
00:21:04,100 --> 00:21:12,400
When emotions arise, instead of stifling them, pay attention to them and try to understand the messages they are trying to deliver.

138
00:21:12,400 --> 00:21:26,100
This way you make use of your emotions without losing your balance and inner stability.

139
00:21:26,100 --> 00:21:44,800
Q19. What does the speaker say about hiding one's emotions?

140
00:21:44,800 --> 00:22:03,080
Q20. What should we do first in dealing with emotions?

141
00:22:03,080 --> 00:22:21,900
Q21. What are we advised to do when emotions arise?

142
00:22:21,900 --> 00:22:23,120
RECORDING 3
Good morning, class.

143
00:22:23,120 --> 00:22:26,320
My topic today is how to feed a hungry world.

144
00:22:26,320 --> 00:22:30,320
The world's population is expected to grow from 6.

145
00:22:30,320 --> 00:22:32,620
8 billion today to 9.

146
00:22:32,620 --> 00:22:35,260
1 billion by 2050.

147
00:22:35,260 --> 00:22:44,500
Meanwhile, the world's population more than doubled from 3 billion between 1961 and 2007.

148
00:22:44,500 --> 00:22:50,160
Simultaneously, food production has been constrained by a lack of scientific research.

149
00:22:50,160 --> 00:22:58,200
Still, the task of feeding the world's population in 2050 seems "easily possible".

150
00:22:58,200 --> 00:23:01,800
What is needed is a second green revolution.

151
00:23:01,800 --> 00:23:08,680
This is an approach that is described as the sustainable growth of global agriculture.

152
00:23:08,680 --> 00:23:16,040
Such a revolution will require a wholesale shift of priorities in agricultural research.

153
00:23:16,040 --> 00:23:19,260
There is an urgent need for new crop varieties.

154
00:23:19,260 --> 00:23:27,820
They must offer higher yields, but use less water, nitrogen-rich fertilizers or other inputs.

155
00:23:27,820 --> 00:23:33,500
These new crops must also be more resistant to drought, heat and pests.

156
00:23:33,500 --> 00:23:43,560
Equally crucial is lower-tech research into basics such as crop rotation and mixed farming of animals and plants on small farms.

157
00:23:43,560 --> 00:23:53,740
Developing nations could score substantial gains in productivity by making better use of modern technologies and practices.

158
00:23:53,740 --> 00:23:55,720
But that requires money.

159
00:23:55,720 --> 00:24:04,980
It is estimated that to meet the 2050 challenge, investment must double to $83 billion US dollars a year.

160
00:24:04,980 --> 00:24:10,080
Most of that money needs to go towards improving agricultural infrastructure.

161
00:24:10,080 --> 00:24:14,960
Everything from production to storage and processing must improve.

162
00:24:14,960 --> 00:24:23,020
However, research agendas need to be focused on the needs of the poorest and most resource-limited countries.

163
00:24:23,020 --> 00:24:31,880
It is there that most of the world's population lives, and it is there that population growth over the next decades will be the greatest.

164
00:24:31,880 --> 00:24:37,660
To their credit, the world's agricultural scientists are embracing such a broad view.

165
00:24:37,660 --> 00:24:52,840
In March, for example, they came together at the first Global Conference on Agricultural Research to begin working out how to change research agendas to help meet the needs of farmers in poorer nations.

166
00:24:52,840 --> 00:25:00,180
But these plans will not bear fruit unless they get considerably more support from policy-makers.

167
00:25:00,180 --> 00:25:08,300
The growth in public agricultural-research spending peaked in the 1970s and has been shrinking ever since.

168
00:25:08,300 --> 00:25:15,700
The big exception is China, where spending has far surpassed other countries over the past decade.

169
00:25:15,700 --> 00:25:23,160
China seems set to transition to become the key supplier of relevant science and technology to poorer countries.

170
00:25:23,160 --> 00:25:28,380
But developed countries have a humanitarian responsibility, too.

171
00:25:28,380 --> 00:25:45,240
Calls by scientists for large increases in the appropriation of funds for public spending on agricultural research are more than justified.

172
00:25:45,240 --> 00:26:05,520
Q22. What is an urgent need for feeding the world's population in 2050, according to the speaker?

173
00:26:05,520 --> 00:26:25,580
Q23. Where should most of the money be invested to feed the ever-growing population?

174
00:26:25,580 --> 00:26:45,440
Q24. Why does the speaker give credit to the world's agricultural scientists?

175
00:26:45,440 --> 00:27:06,604
Q25. What makes China exceptional in comparison with the rest of the world?
